3. Solving Ubuntu/Linux Specifics (Machine-ID)
If you are cloning/restoring Ubuntu (22.04+), DHCP may use the machine ID (/etc/machine-id) instead of the MAC address to assign IP addresses, causing clones to take the same IP. 

    Fix: Before shutting down the VM to create the backup, run:
    bash

truncate -s 0 /etc/machine-id
rm /var/lib/dbus/machine-id
ln -s /etc/machine-id /var/lib/dbus/machine-id


🔐 1. Regenerate SSH host keys (Linux VM)

Run on the cloned VM:
sudo rm -f /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*
sudo dpkg-reconfigure openssh-server

If dpkg-reconfigure is not available (or on some distros), use:
sudo ssh-keygen -A

Then restart SSH:
sudo systemctl restart ssh

Some systems also reuse identity files.

Check and reset machine-id:
sudo rm -f /etc/machine-id
sudo systemd-machine-id-setup

After cloning VM:

Change hostname
Regenerate SSH keys (steps above)
Ensure DHCP/static IP is unique
Optionally regenerate /etc/hostname + /etc/hosts

Example:

sudo hostnamectl set-hostname new-vm-name

